Subject: [#94-11-75] CONSPIRACY: Is HotWired ashamed of its advertising?

 Dave Krieger <dkrieger@netcom.com> wrote: 
>
>FORWARD AND REPOST THIS MESSAGE AS FAR AND WIDE AS YOU CAN...
>

I saw your comments and corrections to this before I saw the original....
>
>Maybe HotWired should be ashamed of its advertising, after all.  Any
>competent economist can tell you what will happen to the quality of content
>in a medium where those who pay the bills are "pushing" the information as
>"sponsors", not "pulling" it as users.  Crap (remember Sturgeon's Law) that
>appeals to the lowest common denominator grabs all the advertisers (because
>it has all the audience), and quality material (like the content of
>HotWired, which is actually quite good) starves, unable to compete with the
>free junk.  Advertisers also shy away from sponsoring anything that might
>offend anybody anywhere, so controversy, diversity, dissent, and choice all
>go away, and the Net becomes another "vast wasteland" populated by "Green
>Acres" and "COPS".  This becomes a real danger when the introduction of new
>providers to the Net is artificially restricted by some form of limit to
>entry, such as Al Gore's expressed desire to make Net providers screen
>their content for "suitability for children", introducing the Net
>equivalent of Hollywood's Hays Office.

Actually, I wonder if the Net will also make advertisers less frightened--
if there will be more chance to appeal to the people who like what you
have to offer, and thus less need to worry about offending everyone
else.

(This only applies to the current anarchic sort of Net--not a government
-controlled Infohighway.)

Still, the great fortunes will be made by selling to the middle of the
bell curve....by definition, you can't make as much selling to the
people with the most intelligence, taste, or even the most money.

I believe that the next round of great fortunes will be made through
nanotechnology by selling to people who are now considered very poor.
It will be something like the way Industrial Revolution vastly increased
the size of the middle class. 

On the other hand, I've seen one promising sign that the bell curve
may not rule forever. I've talked with someone who does commercial
research, and she's discovered that what kind of travel people do
doesn't correlate with their demographics....people are just following
their own tastes.  

Nancy Lebovitz
