From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 04 Nov 94 02:13:41 EST
Subject: [#94-11-78] Neural Darwinism, Ryle, Adler

Luciana Haill wrote 11/3/94: <Gilbert Ryle and Bf Skinner in 1949 assaulted
Cartesian doctrine, giving rise to A.I, where/how was this done ?  What was the
book written on Neural Darwinism by Edelman...  Adler's concepts of patterns and
metaphor ?>

Check out Alfred Adler's holistic view of humans as active agents within the
larger world in "Understanding Human Nature" (1927) (I'll make an exception for
him, maybe not all psychologists are clueless).  Along these lines, you might
look at Edward Tolman's book on cognitive maps in "Purposive Behavior in Animals
and Men" (1932).  Gilbert Ryle's book "The Concept of Mind" (1949), is an
important step in the development of the notion of cognitive maps.  The
Cartesian doctrine, however, was subsumed in 1929 by Jose Ortega y Gasset, who
laid out the current biological truth of "knower-and-known" holism in rigorous
detail.  See his book "What is Philosophy?" (1960 ed.).  Gerald Edelman's book
for the non-scientific  reader is "Bright Air, Brilliant Fire:  On the Matter of
the Mind" (1992), for scientists see his:  "Neural Darwinism:  The Theory of
Neuronal Group Selection" (1987), "Topobiology:  An Introduction to Molecular
Embryology" (1988), and "The Remembered Present:  A Biological Theory of
Consciousness" (1989).

